{
  "context": "In standard Western music theory, a triad is classified by its root, third, and fifth. The quality of a triad in root position is determined by the number of semitones between the root (the lowest note) and its other two notes:\n- Major: 4 semitones from root to third, 7 semitones from root to fifth.\n- Minor: 3 semitones from root to third, 7 semitones from root to fifth.\n- Diminished: 3 semitones from root to third, 6 semitones from root to fifth.\n- Augmented: 4 semitones from root to third, 8 semitones from root to fifth.\nThe standard 12-tone chromatic scale sequence of ascending semitones is C, C#, D, D#, E, F, F#, G, G#, A, A#, B, repeating at the octave. Consider a root-position triad whose notes are, from lowest to highest pitch, F#, A, and C, where each successive note is the closest instance of that pitch class above the previous note.",
  "question": "Based on the provided rules and chromatic scale, what is the quality classification of this triad?",
  "correct_answer": "Diminished",
  "distractors": [
    "Minor",
    "Major",
    "Augmented"
  ]
}

